**You moved from India to China in 2003 to start what became I-Field. What was the original**
**brief?**

There wasn't one, really — that was the point. I saw hotel owners across the region being
sold fit-out and furniture by people who had never run a hotel themselves. I wanted to build
a company that understood the operational side, not just the design brief.

**Why put money into your own factory in 2012, rather than keep sourcing from outside**
**manufacturers?**

Control. When you don't own the factory, every quality problem is a negotiation. When you
do, it's a conversation with your own team the next morning. That single decision changed
what we could promise clients.

**You still visit sites personally. Why, at this scale?**

Because a drawing never tells you everything. I want to see the finish under the actual
lighting the guest will see it under, not the lighting in a sample room. It also means the
site team knows the owner is paying attention, which changes how carefully everyone works.

**What's the hardest lesson nine offices has taught you?**

That the same specification does not travel the same way through nine different regulatory
environments, currencies and labour markets. Consistency has to be engineered into the
process, not assumed from good intentions.
